(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-11-08"] = {"chem-11-08-b01":{"idea":"A homologous series is a family of organic compounds that grow by one unit at a time.","steps":["All members share one general formula, for example CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ for alkanes.","They have the same functional group, so their chemical properties are similar.","Each member differs from the next by one –CH₂– unit.","Their physical properties, such as boiling point, change in a regular way."],"others":"Made only of C and H in one chain is too narrow, since alcohols also form a series. Same molecular formula but different structures describes isomers. Boiling points are not equal; they rise along the series.","answer":"Compounds with one general formula and similar properties, each differing from the next by -CH2-"},"chem-11-08-b02":{"idea":"Alkanes are saturated: every carbon bond that can hold hydrogen does.","steps":["Alkanes have only C–C single bonds and no ring.","Methane is CH₄, ethane is C₂H₆ and propane is C₃H₈.","For n carbons the hydrogens are 2n + 2.","So the general formula is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂."],"others":"CₙH₂ₙ₊₁ is the formula of an alkyl group, not a full molecule. CₙH₂ₙ is the alkene formula. CₙH₂ₙ₋₂ is the alkyne formula.","answer":"CnH2n+2"},"chem-11-08-b03":{"idea":"Each double bond removes two hydrogens from the alkane formula.","steps":["Start with the alkane CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","One C=C double bond takes away 2 H.","So alkenes have the formula CₙH₂ₙ. Ethene is C₂H₄."],"others":"CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ is for alkanes. CₙH₂ₙ₋₂ is for alkynes, which lose 4 H. CₙH₂ₙ₋₁ does not fit any simple neutral series.","answer":"CnH2n"},"chem-11-08-b04":{"idea":"A triple bond removes four hydrogens from the alkane formula.","steps":["Start with CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","One C≡C triple bond counts as two degrees of unsaturation, so 4 H are lost.","So alkynes have CₙH₂ₙ₋₂. Ethyne is C₂H₂."],"others":"CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ is for alkanes. CₙH₂ₙ is for alkenes. CₙH₂ₙ₋₄ would need two triple bonds or equivalent.","answer":"CnH2n-2"},"chem-11-08-b05":{"idea":"Isomers are different compounds with the same atoms in different arrangements.","steps":["Isomers have the same molecular formula.","Their atoms are joined or arranged differently.","So their structures, and often their properties, are different."],"others":"Different formulas cannot make isomers. Identical formula and identical structure is one compound, not two. Isomers are closely related, so no relationship at all is wrong.","answer":"The same molecular formula but different structures"},"chem-11-08-b06":{"idea":"IUPAC names use a suffix to show the main functional group.","steps":["The –COOH group is a carboxylic acid.","Its suffix is –oic acid, so CH₃COOH is ethanoic acid.","The other suffixes belong to other families."],"others":"–oyl is used for acyl chlorides. –oate is used for esters. –amide is used for amides.","answer":"-oic acid, as in ethanoic acid"},"chem-11-08-b07":{"idea":"The –OH group on a saturated carbon makes an alcohol.","steps":["The suffix for an alcohol is –ol.","CH₃CH₂OH has two carbons, so it is ethanol."],"others":"–al is for aldehydes. –one is for ketones. –oic acid is for carboxylic acids.","answer":"-ol"},"chem-11-08-b08":{"idea":"The root of an IUPAC name tells how many carbons are in the main chain.","steps":["Roots: meth = 1, eth = 2, prop = 3, but = 4, pent = 5, hex = 6.","A 4-carbon chain has the root but."],"others":"Prop means 3 carbons, pent means 5 and hex means 6.","trap":"Learn: Meth, Eth, Prop, But, Pent, Hex.","answer":"but"},"chem-11-08-b09":{"idea":"Substitution means swapping one piece for another.","steps":["One atom or group leaves the molecule.","A different atom or group takes its place.","Example: CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl. One H is replaced by Cl."],"others":"Two molecules joining across a multiple bond is addition. Rearranging atoms inside one molecule is rearrangement. Breaking into smaller pieces is elimination or decomposition.","answer":"One atom or group in a molecule is replaced by another atom or group"},"chem-11-08-b10":{"idea":"Addition happens at double or triple bonds.","steps":["The reagent splits and its parts attach to the two carbons of the multiple bond.","The double bond becomes a single bond.","Example: CH₂=CH₂ + Br₂ → CH₂Br–CH₂Br."],"others":"Splitting into two with a new double bond is not addition. Replacement with no change in bonds is substitution. Joining while losing water is condensation.","answer":"Atoms or groups add across a multiple bond, which becomes a single bond"},"chem-11-08-b11":{"idea":"A free radical is a very reactive particle with an odd electron.","steps":["A covalent bond can break so that each atom keeps one electron. This is homolytic fission.","Each fragment has one unpaired electron.","A species with an unpaired electron is a free radical."],"others":"A closed-shell molecule has no unpaired electron. Charged species with no unpaired electrons are ions, not radicals.","answer":"A species with an unpaired electron, formed by homolytic bond cleavage"},"chem-11-08-b12":{"idea":"Alkyl groups push electrons towards a positive carbon and make it more stable.","steps":["More alkyl groups give more +I effect and more hyperconjugation.","A tertiary carbocation has 3 alkyl groups, a secondary has 2, a primary has 1.","The methyl cation has none.","So the order is tertiary > secondary > primary > methyl."],"others":"Carbocations are not equally stable. The reverse order has the trend upside down. The order starting with secondary puts tertiary in the wrong place.","answer":"Tertiary > Secondary > Primary > Methyl"},"chem-11-08-b13":{"idea":"Open-chain (acyclic) compounds have no ring.","steps":["The carbon chain has free ends.","It may be straight or branched.","Cyclohexane is a ring, so it is not acyclic."],"others":"A ring of three or more carbons is a cyclic compound. A heteroatom in the chain is not the test. Acyclic compounds can also have double bonds, so only single bonds is wrong.","answer":"Has no ring; the carbon chain has open ends, branched or not"},"chem-11-08-b14":{"idea":"Degree of unsaturation counts rings plus pi bonds.","steps":["DoU = (2C + 2 − H) ÷ 2.","For C₄H₈: (2 × 4 + 2 − 8) ÷ 2.","= (10 − 8) ÷ 2 = 1.","With no ring, this one unit is a C=C double bond."],"answer":"1"},"chem-11-08-b15":{"idea":"An alkane is fully saturated, so it has no ring or pi bond.","steps":["DoU = (2C + 2 − H) ÷ 2.","For C₄H₁₀: (2 × 4 + 2 − 10) ÷ 2.","= (10 − 10) ÷ 2 = 0."],"answer":"0"},"chem-11-08-b16":{"idea":"Isomers that differ in the carbon skeleton show chain isomerism.","steps":["n-Butane has a straight 4-carbon chain.","2-Methylpropane has a 3-carbon chain with a branch.","The formula is C₄H₁₀ for both, but the skeleton differs.","This is chain isomerism."],"others":"Functional group isomerism needs different functional groups. Position isomerism needs the same skeleton with a group in a different place. Metamerism needs the same functional group with different groups on each side.","answer":"Chain isomerism"},"chem-11-08-b17":{"idea":"If the skeleton stays the same and a group moves, it is position isomerism.","steps":["Both compounds have the same straight C₄ chain.","Cl is on carbon 1 in one and on carbon 2 in the other.","Only the position of the substituent changes.","This is position isomerism."],"others":"Functional group isomerism needs different functional groups. Chain isomerism needs different skeletons. Metamerism needs a group such as O or NH between different alkyl groups.","answer":"Position isomerism"},"chem-11-08-b18":{"idea":"Different functional groups with the same formula give functional group isomerism.","steps":["Both have the formula C₂H₆O.","CH₃CH₂OH has an –OH group, so it is an alcohol.","CH₃–O–CH₃ has a C–O–C link, so it is an ether.","The functional groups differ."],"others":"Position isomerism and chain isomerism keep the same functional group. Metamerism needs the same functional group too.","answer":"Functional group isomerism"},"chem-11-08-b19":{"idea":"Metamerism compares members of one family that split carbons differently around the same group.","steps":["Both compounds are ethers with the formula C₄H₁₀O.","Diethyl ether has 2 + 2 carbons around O.","Methyl propyl ether has 1 + 3 carbons around O.","This is metamerism."],"others":"Chain isomerism changes the skeleton. Functional group isomerism changes the functional group. Position isomerism changes where a group sits on a chain.","answer":"Metamerism"},"chem-11-08-b20":{"idea":"Electron-withdrawing atoms make the acid anion more stable.","steps":["Cl is electronegative and pulls electrons by the –I effect.","This spreads the negative charge of the carboxylate ion.","More Cl atoms give a more stable anion and a stronger acid.","So the order is CCl₃COOH > CHCl₂COOH > CH₂ClCOOH > CH₃COOH."],"others":"They are not equally acidic. Trichloroacetic acid is the strongest, not the weakest. The reverse order puts the acids upside down.","answer":"Trichloroacetic acid > dichloroacetic acid > chloroacetic acid > acetic acid"},"chem-11-08-b21":{"idea":"Groups that push electrons make an acid weaker.","steps":["CH₃ in acetic acid pushes electrons by the +I effect.","This adds negative charge to the acetate ion and makes it less stable.","Formic acid has H instead of CH₃, so its anion is more stable.","So formic acid is the stronger acid."],"others":"CH₃ pushes electrons, not pulls them. Formic acid has no extra C=O group. Bond strength does not depend on the weight of the molecule in this way.","answer":"CH3 in acetic acid pushes electrons (+I), destabilising its anion; formic acid has no alkyl group"},"chem-11-08-b22":{"idea":"The nitro group pulls electrons from a benzene ring through resonance.","steps":["The N atom in –NO₂ is positive and the oxygens are electronegative.","Pi electrons of the ring can move onto the oxygens.","So electron density is withdrawn from the ring.","This is a –M (negative mesomeric) effect."],"others":"+M would mean donating electrons, which –NO₂ does not do. It acts through resonance as well as induction. It does affect the ring strongly.","answer":"-M effect: it withdraws electron density from the ring via resonance"},"chem-11-08-b23":{"idea":"Hyperconjugation spreads positive charge by overlap with nearby C–H bonds.","steps":["A carbocation has an empty p orbital.","A neighbouring C–H sigma bond overlaps with it.","Electron density moves into the empty orbital.","The positive charge is spread, so the cation is more stable."],"others":"Electrons do not flow from the empty p orbital, which has none. Hyperconjugation is not the inductive effect renamed. Overlap of two pi bonds is conjugation.","answer":"Electron density from an adjacent C-H sigma bond spreads into the empty p orbital, easing the positive charge"},"chem-11-08-b24":{"idea":"Elimination removes atoms from neighbouring carbons to form a multiple bond.","steps":["With alcoholic KOH, an alkyl halide loses H from one carbon and X from the next.","A new C=C bond forms.","Example: CH₃CH₂Br + KOH → CH₂=CH₂ + KBr + H₂O."],"others":"Swapping one atom for another is substitution. Joining two molecules with no loss is addition polymerisation. Atoms adding across a double bond is addition.","answer":"Atoms leave adjacent carbons and a new multiple bond forms, e.g. an alkene"},"chem-11-08-b25":{"idea":"In a rearrangement, a group shifts to make the carbocation more stable.","steps":["A hydrogen or an alkyl group moves from a neighbouring carbon.","It moves to the carbon with the positive charge.","The charge ends up on a carbon that is better stabilised.","The molecular formula does not change."],"others":"Losing H⁺ to form a double bond is elimination. A shift that gives a less stable cation does not happen. Gaining a new group changes the formula, so it is not rearrangement.","answer":"A carbocation shifts H or an alkyl group from a neighbouring carbon to become more stable"},"chem-11-08-b26":{"idea":"Count distinct carbon skeletons that fit the formula.","steps":["C₄H₁₀ is an alkane, so there are no rings or double bonds.","Skeleton 1: the straight chain, n-butane.","Skeleton 2: a 3-carbon chain with a methyl branch, 2-methylpropane.","No other skeleton exists for 4 carbons."],"trap":"Do not count the same molecule twice.","answer":"2"},"chem-11-08-b27":{"idea":"Draw straight, one-branch and two-branch skeletons for 5 carbons.","steps":["Straight chain: pentane.","One methyl branch on a 4-carbon chain: 2-methylbutane.","Two methyl groups on one carbon of a 3-carbon chain: 2,2-dimethylpropane.","A methyl on carbon 3 of a 4-carbon chain is the same as 2-methylbutane."],"trap":"Draw each one and check whether it can be turned into another.","answer":"3"},"chem-11-08-b28":{"idea":"Count the different kinds of hydrogen in the molecule.","steps":["n-Butane is CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃.","The two end CH₃ groups are equivalent, so they give one product, 1-chlorobutane.","The two middle CH₂ groups are equivalent, so they give one product, 2-chlorobutane.","So there are 2 products."],"answer":"2"},"chem-11-08-b29":{"idea":"Name a chain by counting the carbons.","steps":["CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃ has 4 carbons in a row with no branch.","The root for 4 is but.","An alkane ends in –ane, so the name is butane."],"others":"Propane has 3 carbons. Pentane has 5. 2-Methylpropane is the branched isomer, with a branch.","answer":"Butane"},"chem-11-08-b30":{"idea":"Find the longest chain, then name the branch and its position.","steps":["(CH₃)₃CH has a central CH joined to three CH₃ groups.","The longest chain has 3 carbons, so the parent is propane.","One CH₃ branch sits on carbon 2, so the name is 2-methylpropane."],"others":"2,2-Dimethylpropane has a carbon with four carbon neighbours. 2-Methylbutane has 5 carbons. Butane is the straight chain.","answer":"2-methylpropane"},"chem-11-08-b31":{"idea":"Find the chain, then give the position of Cl.","steps":["CH₃–CHCl–CH₃ has 3 carbons in the chain, so the parent is propane.","Cl is on the middle carbon, which is carbon 2.","So the name is 2-chloropropane."],"others":"2-Chlorobutane has 4 carbons. 1-Chloropropane has Cl on an end carbon. Chloroethane has only 2 carbons.","answer":"2-chloropropane"},"chem-11-08-b32":{"idea":"Reagents are grouped by whether they give or take electron pairs.","steps":["A nucleophile has a lone pair or a negative charge.","It gives that pair to an electron-poor carbon to form a bond.","Examples are OH⁻, CN⁻ and NH₃."],"others":"A free radical has an unpaired electron, so it is not the same. An electron-deficient species that accepts a pair is an electrophile. A species with no charge and no lone pairs cannot donate electrons.","answer":"An electron-rich species that donates an electron pair to form a new bond, e.g. OH-"},"chem-11-08-b33":{"idea":"An electrophile is attracted to electron-rich places.","steps":["It has an empty orbital or a positive charge.","It accepts an electron pair from a nucleophile.","Examples are carbocations and NO₂⁺."],"others":"A species with an unpaired electron is a radical. An electron-rich species that donates a pair is a nucleophile. NH₄⁺ has a full octet and no empty orbital, so not every positive ion is an electrophile.","answer":"An electron-deficient species that accepts an electron pair, e.g. a carbocation or NO2+"},"chem-11-08-b34":{"idea":"Degree of unsaturation counts rings and pi bonds.","steps":["DoU = (2C + 2 − H) ÷ 2.","For C₆H₆: (2 × 6 + 2 − 6) ÷ 2.","= (14 − 6) ÷ 2 = 4.","Check: benzene has 1 ring and 3 double bonds, so 1 + 3 = 4."],"answer":"4"},"chem-11-08-b35":{"idea":"Apply the degree of unsaturation formula to C₆H₁₀.","steps":["DoU = (2C + 2 − H) ÷ 2.","For C₆H₁₀: (2 × 6 + 2 − 10) ÷ 2.","= (14 − 10) ÷ 2 = 2.","Check: cyclohexene has 1 ring and 1 C=C, so 1 + 1 = 2."],"answer":"2"},"chem-11-08-b36":{"idea":"The rule on numbering helps everyone give the same name to one compound.","steps":["First pick the longest chain.","Then number from the end that gives the lowest numbers to the substituents.","This gives one correct name for each compound."],"others":"Numbering does not start near nitrogen as a rule. The number of hydrogens at the end does not matter. Numbering direction does affect the name, so saying it never does is wrong.","answer":"The substituent(s) get the lowest possible locant number(s)"},"chem-11-08-b37":{"idea":"Each member of a homologous series adds one –CH₂– unit.","steps":["Propane is C₃H₈.","Adding CH₂ gives C₄H₁₀.","C₄H₁₀ is butane."],"others":"Pentane is two steps after propane. Methane and ethane come before propane.","answer":"Butane (C4H10)"},"chem-11-08-b38":{"idea":"Free radicals are stabilised in the same way as carbocations.","steps":["Alkyl groups give +I effect and hyperconjugation to the carbon with the odd electron.","More alkyl groups mean more stability.","So tertiary > secondary > primary > methyl."],"others":"Starting with primary or methyl reverses or breaks the trend. Radicals are not all equally stable.","answer":"Tertiary > Secondary > Primary > Methyl"},"chem-11-08-b39":{"idea":"The suffix –al marks an aldehyde.","steps":["Ethanal is CH₃CHO.","The –CHO group is the aldehyde group.","The suffix –al means aldehyde."],"others":"An alcohol ends in –ol. A carboxylic acid ends in –oic acid. A ketone ends in –one.","answer":"Aldehyde"},"chem-11-08-b40":{"idea":"The suffix –one marks a ketone.","steps":["Propan-2-one is CH₃COCH₃.","Its C=O group sits between two carbon groups.","This is a ketone."],"others":"An ether has C–O–C with no C=O. An alcohol has –OH. An aldehyde has C=O at the end of the chain with an H.","answer":"Ketone"},"chem-11-08-b41":{"idea":"Different names do not always mean different compounds.","steps":["Propane has the chain C1–C2–C3, and it is symmetric.","C1 and C3 are the same kind of end carbon.","So 1-chloropropane and 3-chloropropane are the same compound.","The correct name is 1-chloropropane."],"others":"1-Chlorobutane and 2-chlorobutane are different position isomers. Ethanol and dimethyl ether are different functional group isomers. n-Butane and 2-methylpropane are different chain isomers.","trap":"Number from the end that gives the lower number.","answer":"1-chloropropane and 3-chloropropane"},"chem-11-08-b42":{"idea":"Tautomers are isomers that switch quickly between two forms by moving a proton.","steps":["Acetone has a keto form, CH₃COCH₃.","Its enol form is CH₂=C(OH)CH₃.","A proton and a double bond change position.","The two forms are in rapid equilibrium, so this is tautomerism."],"others":"Metamerism is about alkyl groups around the same group. Chain isomerism is about the skeleton. Position isomerism is about where a group sits on a fixed skeleton.","answer":"Tautomerism"},"chem-11-08-b43":{"idea":"The more alkyl groups on the positive carbon, the more stable the cation.","steps":["(CH₃)₃C⁺ has three alkyl groups, so it is tertiary.","(CH₃)₂CH⁺ has two, so it is secondary.","CH₃CH₂CH₂⁺ has one, so it is primary.","Stability order: tertiary > secondary > primary."],"others":"Placing secondary first or primary first breaks the rule. The three are not equally stable.","answer":"(CH3)3C+ > (CH3)2CH+ > CH3-CH2-CH2+"},"chem-11-08-b44":{"idea":"With nitrogen, the formula for unsaturation adds N.","steps":["DoU = (2C + 2 + N − H) ÷ 2.","For C₅H₅N: (2 × 5 + 2 + 1 − 5) ÷ 2.","= 8 ÷ 2 = 4.","Check: pyridine has 1 ring and 3 double bonds, so 1 + 3 = 4."],"answer":"4"},"chem-11-08-b45":{"idea":"A hydride shift changes the carbocation but not the molecular formula.","steps":["A hydrogen with its electron pair moves to the positive carbon.","A secondary cation becomes a tertiary cation.","Moving an atom or group within the molecule is rearrangement."],"others":"Substitution swaps atoms. Addition adds across a multiple bond. Elimination removes atoms to form a multiple bond. None of these describes a shift inside the cation.","answer":"A rearrangement reaction"},"chem-11-08-b46":{"idea":"The inductive effect follows electronegativity.","steps":["F is the most electronegative halogen.","Electronegativity falls down the group: F > Cl > Br > I.","So the –I effect is strongest for F and weakest for I."],"others":"I > Br > Cl > F reverses the order. Cl > F > I > Br is not in electronegativity order. The halogens do not show identical effects.","answer":"F > Cl > Br > I"},"chem-11-08-b47":{"idea":"Add the distinct monochloro products from each C₄H₁₀ skeleton.","steps":["n-Butane gives 1-chlorobutane and 2-chlorobutane, which is 2.","2-Methylpropane gives 1-chloro-2-methylpropane from its 9 primary H.","It also gives 2-chloro-2-methylpropane from its 1 tertiary H, which is 2.","Total = 2 + 2 = 4."],"trap":"Count by types of hydrogen, not by number of hydrogens.","answer":"4"},"chem-11-08-b48":{"idea":"Metamerism needs the same functional group with a different split of carbons around it.","steps":["C₂H₅NHC₂H₅ is a secondary amine with 2 + 2 carbons around NH.","CH₃NHC₃H₇ is also a secondary amine, with 1 + 3 carbons.","The formula is C₄H₁₁N for both.","So this pair shows metamerism."],"others":"The pair with an alcohol and an ether is functional group isomerism. Propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol are position isomers. n-Butane and isobutane are chain isomers.","answer":"C2H5NHC2H5 and CH3NHC3H7: both secondary amines, C4H11N, alkyl groups split differently"},"chem-11-08-b49":{"idea":"Resonance spreads charge over more than one atom and gives extra stability.","steps":["In CH₂=CH–CH₂⁺, the empty p orbital lines up with the C=CH₂ pi bond.","The pi electrons can shift, so the charge moves between two carbons.","Two equivalent resonance forms exist.","This is more stabilising than hyperconjugation alone."],"others":"It is more stable than an ordinary primary cation, not less. The double bond helps through resonance, so the –I explanation is not the main reason. Hyperconjugation alone would make it like an ordinary primary cation.","answer":"Resonance: the positive charge spreads over two carbons through the adjacent pi bond"},"chem-11-08-b50":{"idea":"Same formula but different type of structure is ring-chain isomerism.","steps":["Propene and cyclopropane are both C₃H₆.","Same formula and different structure means they are isomers.","One is an open chain with a C=C. The other is a saturated ring.","This special case is called ring-chain isomerism."],"others":"Chain isomerism compares two open-chain skeletons. A ring compound can share a formula with a chain, so saying they are not isomers is wrong. Metamerism needs a functional group with different alkyl groups.","answer":"Isomers, but ring-chain isomers: one is acyclic with a C=C, the other a saturated ring"}};
